<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arles</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Arelate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arles is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> in Provence, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, near the head of the river's delta. As Roman Arelate it was a leading city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, with an amphitheatre and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> that still stand, and an imperial residence in the 4th century. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> its archbishops were powerful, and it gave its name to the Kingdom of Arles, formed from the Burgundian realms in the 10th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arles.jpg" alt="Arles" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chensiyuan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1_arles_france_2016.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of <b>Arles</b>, often called the Kingdom of Burgundy, was formed by the union of two kingdoms that had emerged from the break-up of the Carolingian Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-arles">Kingdom of Arles · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Theodoric I (418–451) attacked <b>Arles</b> and Narbonne, was beaten by Litorius's Hunnic mercenaries in 436, but won the Battle of Toulouse against Romans and Huns in 439.</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The first council of <b>Arles</b>, the first called by Constantine, was opened by presiding bishop Marinus a year after the Edict of Milan.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-1">1 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After a quarrel with Paul Gauguin in <b>Arles</b>, Vincent van Gogh cut off part of his left ear.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-23">23 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>At <b>Arles</b>, Ramon Berenguer III, Count of Barcelona, married Douce I, who had that year inherited the county of Provence through her mother.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-3">3 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Backed by the Visigoths and the Gallo-Roman nobility, the senator Avitus was acclaimed emperor near <b>Arles</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-9">9 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
